{% aside type="caution" title="In development" %}
These guidelines will grow as model training matures. How trained models are
submitted, hosted, and licensed has not been decided yet.
{% /aside %}

## Set up the tools

Until `nahpu_dl` is published, milu builds against a local copy of the NAHPU
API checked out next to it.

{% steps %}

1. Install the latest stable [Rust toolchain](https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install).

2. Clone both repositories into the same parent directory.

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/hhandika/milu.git
   ```

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu_api.git
   ```

   milu expects the NAHPU API at `../nahpu_api`.

3. Build and check milu.

   ```bash
   cargo build --release
   ```

   Run `milu --help` to list all commands and options.

{% /steps %}

## Prepare data

- Use only data you have permission to share. Synthetic or anonymized samples
  are welcome for examples and tests.
- Keep a holdout set the model never sees during training, and evaluate it
  with `milu test`.
- For tables, set the column to predict with `-t` and an identifier column
  with `--id-column`, so predictions can be traced back to specimens.
- For NAHPU data packages, choose the label rank with `--rank`, for example
  `genus`.

## Share a model

- Share the whole package directory: `manifest.toml` and its weight file.
- Keep the manifest's `provenance` and `metrics` so others know where the
  model came from and how well it performed.
- Run `milu models info` on the package before you share it.

## Contribute code

milu is the command line front end. Changes to models, data loading,
fine-tuning, or weight storage belong in `nahpu_dl` in the
[NAHPU API](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu_api). Read the `AGENTS.md` file of
each repository before you start. In short:

- Rust edition 2024 in a Cargo workspace. Keep `main.rs` thin: argument
  parsing goes in `cli/`, and logic goes in `core/`.
- Return `Result` and propagate errors with `?`. Use `anyhow` with context in
  application code and `thiserror` in library crates. Avoid `unwrap()` outside
  tests.
- Log with the `log` macros instead of `println!` in library code.
- Build paths with `Path::join` so the code works on Linux, macOS, and
  Windows.
- Keep dependencies minimal and aim for a single self-contained executable.
- Document every public item, and put unit tests next to the code they test.

Before opening a pull request, run:

```bash
cargo fmt --all
```

```bash
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
```

```bash
cargo test --workspace
```

When behavior changes for users, add an entry under "Upcoming Release" in
`CHANGELOG.md`. See [Become a contributor](/en/contributing/) for other ways
to help.
